  "brief_description": "Charlotte Cuhrt was 15 years old when Max Pechstein painted this striking full-length portrait. The daughter of Max Cuhrt, a successful solicitor and patron of the avant-garde, she sits confidently in an armchair, her big black eyes looking directly at the viewer. She’s dressed in red, with a large, dark hat on her head and a flamboyant ring on her left hand. Displayed in an altar-like, custom-made wooden frame – an artwork in itself – the picture was part of a wider decorative scheme for the Cuhrts’ lavish apartment in Kurfürstendamm 152, Berlin.\n\nPechstein was, in 1910, at the peak of his career. After a conventional artistic training at the Royal Academy of Dresden, he took up a dramatic, Expressionist style, profoundly influenced by the art of Vincent van Gogh, Edvard Munch and the Fauves. In 1906 he joined the artistic group Die Brücke (The Bridge), a key movement in the development of German Expressionism.",
